    "Unit": {
      "description": "What one of the metric's numbers counts.\n\n**The five money and count members were added on 2026-08-30**, when the nineteen KPIs\nof `semantic.subscription_daily_metrics` entered the catalog. Declaring `users` for a\nfigure in reais would have been false, and the enum existing in a shape that forces a\nfalse answer is not a reason to give one.\n\n``COUNT`` is deliberately vague and that vagueness is measured: for `Sales (qty)`,\n`Cancellations (qty)`, `Not renewed (qty)` and `Chargeback (qty)` the view declares a\ncount and **does not declare what is counted**. The thing counted is a named gap in\neach contract, not a guess here.",
      "enum": [
        "users",
        "sessions",
        "events",
        "rate",
        "stars",
        "count",
        "subscriptions",
        "months",
        "currency_brl",
        "currency_usd"
      ],
      "title": "Unit",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "ValueColumn": {
      "description": "Which column of the source view carries this metric's number — `FR-806`.\n\n**This exists because of a published error.** MRR and Revenue were reported as having\nno value, from reading only `value`; they do have values and they live in `value_usd`.\nSo the column is DECLARED per metric and read from the declaration, and above all it is\n**never defaulted to `value`** — that default is precisely what produced the error.\n\nThe rule an author follows is ``usd_*`` -> ``value_usd``, ``brl`` -> ``value_brl``,\neverything else -> ``value``. **That rule governs the author, not this enum**: written\ninto code it would be a second copy of a fact the contracts already carry, and two\ncopies of one fact disagree the day one of them moves.",
      "enum": [
        "value",
        "value_usd",
        "value_brl"
      ],
      "title": "ValueColumn",
      "type": "string"
    }
